### Revshare simulator — live demo walkthrough
#
# A fixed, rehearsed script for showing the domain rules that are easy to get wrong:
# the anniversary cap year, a closing that straddles the cap, the flat post-cap fee,
# idempotent replay, forfeiture eligibility, and a sponsorship hierarchy that doesn't
# compress on departure.
#
# Every request in this file has been run end to end against real Postgres, Kafka and
# Mongo containers, and every dollar figure in the comments is copied from an actual
# response, not hand-derived. If you ever regenerate the ids or change a date, requests
# 4 through 7 need their numbers re-verified by actually running them — see the comment
# on each for what depends on what.
#
# Setup:
#   export JAVA_HOME=$(/usr/libexec/java_home -v 21)
#   docker compose up -d
#   ./mvnw -pl commission-service spring-boot:run &                                                  # :8080
#   ./mvnw -pl reporting-service spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.arguments=--server.port=8081 &     # :8081
#
# Run top to bottom, in order — several requests depend on the state the ones above left behind.
# Works with the VS Code "REST Client" extension or IntelliJ's built-in HTTP client.
#
# If a dashboard GET 404s right after you start reporting-service: a Kafka consumer
# group can take a few seconds to settle its coordinator election on a freshly started
# broker. Give it a moment and retry rather than assuming the projection is broken.

@commission = http://localhost:8080
@reporting = http://localhost:8081

@agentA = 28a3cfd8-02ab-4e37-a901-a123d9ae8182
@agentB = 4b7756bf-716f-479f-8a9f-1c46449f3163
@agentC = 282cf72a-ef4c-4ea7-8b79-32c83dcd6f12

@txBPriming = 53253636-fcb2-4f12-be44-03e45d4b5e0f
@tx1 = 95108d99-b71f-4870-8180-0332679bb4c8
@tx2 = 310be8fc-cd2d-4b2c-98d8-87486ebf063a
@tx3 = 28007f9f-b671-4e6b-829c-e8996c86cb3c


### 1. Enroll Agent A — top of the tree, no sponsor
# sponsorId is simply omitted. The path is derived server-side, never accepted from
# the client — that's what makes every tier and revenue-share rate downstream
# trustworthy.
POST {{commission}}/agents
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "agentId": "{{agentA}}",
  "firstName": "Ada",
  "lastName": "Ancestor",
  "email": "ada.ancestor@revshare.test",
  "joinedOn": "2022-01-10"
}


### 2. Enroll Agent B — sponsored by A
POST {{commission}}/agents
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "agentId": "{{agentB}}",
  "firstName": "Bo",
  "lastName": "Bridge",
  "email": "bo.bridge@revshare.test",
  "joinedOn": "2022-02-01",
  "sponsorId": "{{agentA}}"
}


### 3. Enroll Agent C — sponsored by B, three deep from A
# A <- B <- C.
POST {{commission}}/agents
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "agentId": "{{agentC}}",
  "firstName": "Cy",
  "lastName": "Closer",
  "email": "cy.closer@revshare.test",
  "joinedOn": "2022-03-15",
  "sponsorId": "{{agentB}}"
}


### 4. B closes one of their own — makes B a real, producing beneficiary
# Revenue share only pays a beneficiary who is themselves selling: the Producing Agent
# Policy needs $450 of gross commission in the trailing six months. Skip this request
# and B still shows up in every response below, but every award to B comes back
# forfeited with BENEFICIARY_NOT_PRODUCING — worth doing once on purpose so you can
# show that failure mode too, but this script shows the paid path.
#
# This closing's own revenue share goes up to A (tier 1 relative to B, since B's
# upline is just A) — and forfeits, because A has never closed anything either:
# entitlement 250.00, awarded 0.00, forfeitReason BENEFICIARY_NOT_PRODUCING. That's
# expected, not a second bug to chase.
POST {{commission}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionId": "{{txBPriming}}",
  "agentId": "{{agentB}}",
  "closedOn": "2023-05-01",
  "salePrice": "150000.00",
  "grossCommissionIncome": "5000.00",
  "side": "LISTING",
  "propertyReference": "PROP-DEMO-0001"
}


### 5. Baseline closing for C — well under the cap
# C's cap year runs 2023-03-15 to 2024-03-14 (their anniversary, not the calendar).
# Company share = 73333.33 x 15% = 10999.9995, rounds HALF_UP to $11,000.00 — chosen
# so C's cap balance lands at exactly $1,000 remaining after this one.
#
# Because B is now producing (request 4, within the trailing six months of this
# closing), B's tier-1 award actually pays: entitlement 3666.67, awarded 3666.67. A's
# tier-2 award still forfeits — TIER_LOCKED, not BENEFICIARY_NOT_PRODUCING — because
# tier 2 needs five producing frontline agents under A, and a 3-agent demo can't
# supply that. Say that out loud before it looks like a bug.
POST {{commission}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionId": "{{tx1}}",
  "agentId": "{{agentC}}",
  "closedOn": "2023-06-01",
  "salePrice": "950000.00",
  "grossCommissionIncome": "73333.33",
  "side": "LISTING",
  "propertyReference": "PROP-DEMO-0002"
}


### 6. The straddle — $1,000 of cap left, a $20,000 closing arrives
# Normal split would take $3,000 (15%); only $1,000 of cap remains, so the company
# takes $1,000 and the other $2,000 goes to the agent instead of being carried
# forward. pricedUnderPostCapFee is false (still priced under the split — the fee
# starts on the *next* closing) and reachedCap is true. revenueShareEligibleGross
# reads $6,666.67, not $20,000 — only the pre-cap slice funds the upline.
#
# The number worth pausing on: B's tier-1 award here is entitlement 333.33, awarded
# 333.33 — and 3666.67 + 333.33 = $4,000.00 exactly, which is tier 1's annual maximum
# per contributor, exhausted in the exact closing that caps C. That's not a
# coincidence: both figures are the same $80,000 of gross-equivalent commission
# ($73,333.33 + the $6,666.67 pre-cap slice of this one), read through a different
# rate. See the README's "the numbers are derived, not typed in" section — this is
# that derivation showing up live. A's forfeited total after this closing will land
# on exactly $3,200.00 for the identical reason, on tier 2's 4% instead.
POST {{commission}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionId": "{{tx2}}",
  "agentId": "{{agentC}}",
  "closedOn": "2023-07-15",
  "salePrice": "260000.00",
  "grossCommissionIncome": "20000.00",
  "side": "DUAL",
  "propertyReference": "PROP-DEMO-0003"
}


### 7. Post-cap — the flat fee kicks in
# C capped on the closing above, so this one skips the split entirely: a flat $285
# (non-Elite) instead of 15%. capContribution is $0 and, because none of this gross
# produced company dollar, revenueShareEligibleGross is $0 — the awards list comes
# back genuinely empty, not present-and-forfeited. RevenueShareCalculator short-
# circuits before evaluating any beneficiary when there's nothing to distribute.
POST {{commission}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionId": "{{tx3}}",
  "agentId": "{{agentC}}",
  "closedOn": "2023-09-01",
  "salePrice": "195000.00",
  "grossCommissionIncome": "15000.00",
  "side": "BUYING",
  "propertyReference": "PROP-DEMO-0004"
}


### 8. Replay the straddle closing — identical body, same transaction id
# 200, not 201 — the status line alone says "this already happened" without parsing
# the body. revenueShare is null in the response, not a zeroed block: the replay path
# never re-reads the ledger, so it genuinely does not know who was paid, and
# reporting a zero would misstate that as a fact.
POST {{commission}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionId": "{{tx2}}",
  "agentId": "{{agentC}}",
  "closedOn": "2023-07-15",
  "salePrice": "260000.00",
  "grossCommissionIncome": "20000.00",
  "side": "DUAL",
  "propertyReference": "PROP-DEMO-0003"
}


### 9. Terminate B — the mid-chain departure
# B has both an upline (A) and a downline (C) at the moment they leave. Nothing is
# deleted: POST .../termination, not DELETE.
POST {{commission}}/agents/{{agentB}}/termination
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "terminatedOn": "2023-10-01"
}


### 10. Dashboard for A — the hierarchy does not compress
# The moment worth narrating slowly. C stays at tier 2 beneath A, not promoted to
# tier 1 now that B is gone — a naive live-parent-pointer implementation would
# promote C here and pay A the 5% rate instead of the correct 4%. B still shows in
# A's tier 1, marked departed, not removed. A's totalForfeited reads $3,450.00:
# $250.00 from B's own closing (request 4) plus the $3,200.00 tier-2 lock described
# above — every dollar accounted for, none of it paid, all of it explained.
GET {{reporting}}/agents/{{agentA}}/dashboard


### 11. Dashboard for B — a departed agent's earnings are still exactly right
# affiliation.active is false, but revenueShare.totalAwarded reads $4,000.00 —
# tier 1's derived annual maximum, earned before B left and never touched by the
# termination that came after. Nothing here gets deleted or zeroed on departure;
# only the ability to earn *new* awards stops.
GET {{reporting}}/agents/{{agentB}}/dashboard


### 12. Dashboard for C — cap progress and production, from the read side
# Confirms the write side's numbers made it through the outbox -> Kafka -> projection
# pipeline: cap progress against the 2023-03-15 anniversary window, capped and
# cappedOn 2023-07-15, and production totals from all three closings
# ($108,333.33 gross, $96,048.33 to the agent, $12,000.00 to the company, $285.00 in
# post-cap fees).
GET {{reporting}}/agents/{{agentC}}/dashboard


### --- Bonus: edge cases worth having queued up if there's time left ---

### 13. Re-enroll Agent A — 409, not an idempotent replay
# Unlike a closing, an enrolment carries a name and email that might differ from
# what's stored — silently accepting a repeat would risk overwriting a real record
# from what only looks like a retry.
POST {{commission}}/agents
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "agentId": "{{agentA}}",
  "firstName": "Ada",
  "lastName": "Ancestor",
  "email": "ada.ancestor@revshare.test",
  "joinedOn": "2022-01-10"
}


### 14. Terminate B again — 409, the aggregate refuses a second departure date
POST {{commission}}/agents/{{agentB}}/termination
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "terminatedOn": "2023-11-01"
}


### 15. Malformed closing — 400, not a 500
# grossCommissionIncome is missing entirely. This is a request-shape problem
# (presence), handled by validation on the request DTO — distinct from a
# business-rule rejection like gross exceeding sale price, which is enforced by
# ClosedTransaction itself and would also come back as a 400, but from the domain.
POST {{commission}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
  "agentId": "{{agentC}}",
  "closedOn": "2023-09-15",
  "salePrice": "195000.00",
  "side": "BUYING",
  "propertyReference": "PROP-DEMO-0005"
}


### 16. Dashboard for an agent nobody ever enrolled — 404, not an empty 200
# An empty dashboard would assert zero production as fact. 404 says "no such agent"
# instead, which is genuinely true only because AgentEnrolled exists — an agent
# enrolled but never producing gets a real, zeroed dashboard, not this.
GET {{reporting}}/agents/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/dashboard
